```

The check `it >= end` ensures `it < end`, but then `memcpy(&chunk, it, 8)` reads 8 bytes from `it`. Since `end = buffer.size() - 16` (padding_bytes = 16), and `it < end`, we have `it + 8 <= end + 7 < buffer.size()`. So this is safe.

But wait - in the decode loop:
```cpp
std::memcpy(p, start, 8);
```

This reads 8 bytes from `start`. `start` can be up to `it - 1` (since the loop breaks when `start >= it`). And `it` points to the closing quote. So `start + 8 <= it + 7`. Since `it < end` and `end = buffer.size() - 16`, we have `start + 8 <= end + 7 < buffer.size()`. Safe.

